1
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:04,751
Let’s move now to the last part of this
lecture, focusing on bugs and how they can

2
00:00:04,751 --> 00:00:09,000
be exploited by adversaries.
In this case, we will focus on a specific

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:13,270
type of attack, the buffer overflow attack.

4
00:00:13,270 --> 00:00:19,440
Before focusing on buffer overflow attacks,
let me focus on some general considerations.

5
00:00:19,440 --> 00:00:25,010
It should be clear that cracking a password
is only one way to gain control from the attacker's

6
00:00:25,010 --> 00:00:31,510
perspective. There can be alternative ways,
which even require fewer efforts in some cases.

7
00:00:31,510 --> 00:00:37,730
One of them is to manage to trick the user
into doing something that will harm him/her,

8
00:00:37,730 --> 00:00:42,520
and this could be done by infecting the browser,
have users download malicious content, and

9
00:00:42,520 --> 00:00:46,290
so on...
The other is to exploit bugs, which are definitely

10
00:00:46,290 --> 00:00:48,840
there in Operating Systems.

11
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:55,660
Let’s focus now on buffer overflow attacks.
First, we need to know that the C programming

12
00:00:55,660 --> 00:01:01,829
language does not perform array bounds checks.
If we want to be sure we do not write in an

13
00:01:01,829 --> 00:01:07,610
array (a buffer of characters, for instance)
something that is larger than the array itself,

14
00:01:07,610 --> 00:01:13,570
we need to do that explicitly.
Second, we need to remember that the stack

15
00:01:13,570 --> 00:01:20,439
of a process grows downwards, from the last
address to smaller ones.

16
00:01:20,439 --> 00:01:29,450
Let’s see now what is happening.
When we jump from the main function to a different

17
00:01:29,450 --> 00:01:34,870
function, we need to keep track of where we
were, so that we can get back there once the

18
00:01:34,870 --> 00:01:42,740
function has completed its execution. We do
this by writing a return address.

19
00:01:42,740 --> 00:01:49,500
After such a return address, we will have
the various variables used by the function

20
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:54,830
(A in the example).
Among these, we can have a buffer B.

21
00:01:54,830 --> 00:02:02,630
If a malicious user is aware of this and also
has the chance of giving back to the procedure

22
00:02:02,630 --> 00:02:13,849
A what should be written inside B, then it
can give a longer input. This input can overwrite

23
00:02:13,849 --> 00:02:18,430
the return address.
In this case, the attacker can put a predefined

24
00:02:18,430 --> 00:02:28,740
value there, even pointing to an address within
the input he/she just provided.

25
00:02:28,740 --> 00:02:33,750
The code available there would then be executed
next.

26
00:02:33,750 --> 00:02:41,120
It turns out some system calls from the OS
suffer from this bug. If the return address

27
00:02:41,120 --> 00:02:46,570
is changed to point to an injected code that
starts a terminal, a malicious user can have

28
00:02:46,570 --> 00:02:50,730
access with full privileges to the computer
(because the system call was being run by

29
00:02:50,730 --> 00:02:56,599
the operating system itself)

30
00:02:56,599 --> 00:03:03,400
A way to detect possible attacks (that is
not 100% secure) is to use stack canaries.

31
00:03:03,400 --> 00:03:08,060
In this case, just below the return address,
we can add a random value and compile programs

32
00:03:08,060 --> 00:03:12,870
with special code that will stop the execution
if such value is changed.

33
00:03:12,870 --> 00:03:18,540
The idea is that when overflowing the buffer,
the attacker will also change such random

34
00:03:18,540 --> 00:03:22,040
value, and the program will be able to detect
this.

35
00:03:22,040 --> 00:03:29,060
As I mention in the slide, you can check in
the book how stack canaries can be avoided.

36
00:03:29,060 --> 00:03:34,590
Alternative ways to go include the no-execute
bit, which in this case is based on the hardware

37
00:03:34,590 --> 00:03:40,590
itself and used to specify portions of memory
that cannot contain executable parts.

38
00:03:40,590 --> 00:03:45,640
In this case, it is possible to prevent code
injection attacks (because no code will be

39
00:03:45,640 --> 00:03:46,620
executed).

